# Line endings are normalised to LF in the repository and checked out as LF on
# every platform. Without this, a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true
# rewrites text files as CRLF, and anything compared byte-for-byte against a
# file that did not come through git starts failing.
#
# The case that bit us: public/pdfjs/pdf.worker.min.mjs is committed so it can
# be served by URL, and a test asserts it is byte-identical to the copy in
# node_modules — because a parser and a worker from different versions fail at
# run time in the browser and nowhere else. A CRLF checkout broke that test
# while the repository's own copy was correct, which is the worst shape for a
# defect: red locally, invisible in the diff, fine in production.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git never applies any end-of-line conversion to them. Git's own
# detection is a NUL-byte heuristic and it has held so far, but the fixtures it
# protects are the entire evidence base for the citation gate: a PDF or DOCX
# silently altered on checkout would break verbatim matching, which is the one
# property ADR 0001 makes a bug rather than a degraded result.
*.pdf   binary
*.docx  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary

# Minified and generated output. Diffing it helps nobody and it is large enough
# to bury a real change in a review.
*.min.js   -diff
*.min.mjs  -diff
package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated
